WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.582
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level three.

2
00:00:05.882 --> 00:00:09.362
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.662 --> 00:00:15.948
Unit seventeen: Civic Duty. Today you will use gerunds as subjects and after it is no use.

4
00:00:16.248 --> 00:00:18.900
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.867 --> 00:00:22.902
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:24.832
Jury duty.

7
00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:28.722
Jury duty means the legal duty to serve on a jury.

8
00:00:29.317 --> 00:00:32.280
Jury duty brings ordinary citizens into the courtroom.

9
00:00:32.883 --> 00:00:35.396
Your turn. Say: Jury duty.

10
00:00:38.233 --> 00:00:39.316
Jury duty.

11
00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:41.643
Voting responsibility.

12
00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:46.496
Voting responsibility means the duty of citizens to use their vote.

13
00:00:47.083 --> 00:00:50.488
Voting responsibility includes learning about the candidates.

14
00:00:51.083 --> 00:00:54.154
Your turn. Say: Voting responsibility.

15
00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.500
Voting responsibility.

16
00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:00.820
Community service.

17
00:01:01.817 --> 00:01:05.639
Community service means unpaid work done to help a community.

18
00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:08.900
Community service was part of the school programme.

19
00:01:09.483 --> 00:01:12.693
Your turn. Say: Community service.

20
00:01:15.533 --> 00:01:17.185
Community service.

21
00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:19.555
Civic participation.

22
00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:25.011
Civic participation means taking part in the life of your community or country.

23
00:01:25.617 --> 00:01:29.160
Civic participation includes attending public meetings.

24
00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:33.227
Your turn. Say: Civic participation.

25
00:01:36.067 --> 00:01:37.799
Civic participation.

26
00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:39.830
Public engagement.

27
00:01:40.817 --> 00:01:44.697
Public engagement means involving citizens in public decisions.

28
00:01:45.283 --> 00:01:48.641
Public engagement improved after the town meetings began.

29
00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:52.506
Your turn. Say: Public engagement.

30
00:01:55.367 --> 00:01:56.588
Public engagement.

31
00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:58.607
Volunteer work.

32
00:01:59.617 --> 00:02:03.427
Volunteer work means work done freely, without being paid.

33
00:02:04.017 --> 00:02:06.701
Volunteer work at the shelter taught her patience.

34
00:02:07.317 --> 00:02:10.457
Your turn. Say: Volunteer work.

35
00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:14.870
Volunteer work.

36
00:02:15.617 --> 00:02:17.117
Active citizenship.

37
00:02:18.117 --> 00:02:22.136
Active citizenship means taking responsibility for improving society.

38
00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:25.527
Active citizenship begins at the local level.

39
00:02:26.117 --> 00:02:29.326
Your turn. Say: Active citizenship.

40
00:02:32.167 --> 00:02:33.702
Active citizenship.

41
00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:35.869
Social contract.

42
00:02:36.883 --> 00:02:41.379
Social contract means the unwritten agreement between citizens and their society.

43
00:02:41.983 --> 00:02:44.679
The social contract links rights with duties.

44
00:02:45.283 --> 00:02:48.644
Your turn. Say: Social contract.

45
00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:52.919
Social contract.

46
00:02:53.733 --> 00:02:57.291
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:03.287
Visal, a teacher says 'Voting is a responsibility.' Why is 'voting' used as the subject?

48
00:03:04.383 --> 00:03:19.661
A gerund works as a noun. It names an activity as a general idea: Serving on a jury is a duty. Volunteering builds active citizenship. It is more natural than 'To vote is a responsibility'.

49
00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:22.784
Are there fixed expressions with -ing?

50
00:03:23.883 --> 00:03:38.227
Yes: it is worth taking part, it is no use complaining, there is no point in staying silent, and it is not worth ignoring jury duty. After these expressions, the verb is always -ing.

51
00:03:39.317 --> 00:03:40.678
How do I make a negative?

52
00:03:41.783 --> 00:03:54.618
Put not before the gerund: Not voting is also a choice. Not attending community meetings weakens public engagement, because the social contract depends on participation.

53
00:03:55.767 --> 00:03:57.499
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:03.740
Grammar focus: Gerunds as subjects: Voting is and It is no use -ing.

55
00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:05.285
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:05.983 --> 00:04:08.296
Serving on a jury is a civic duty.

57
00:04:09.183 --> 00:04:11.682
It is no use complaining if you never vote.

58
00:04:12.583 --> 00:04:14.920
Not voting weakens the social contract.

59
00:04:15.867 --> 00:04:23.885
A gerund (-ing) can be a subject: Voting is a duty. Use it after it is worth / no use / no point in. Negative: Not + -ing.

60
00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:28.127
Be careful! Don't say: It is no use to complain.

61
00:04:30.067 --> 00:04:32.322
Say: It is no use complaining.

62
00:04:32.622 --> 00:04:34.888
Use -ing after 'it is no use'.

63
00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:38.364
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:39.167 --> 00:04:40.562
What We Owe Each Other.

65
00:04:40.862 --> 00:04:45.207
A healthy society depends on more than laws; it depends on habits.

66
00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:52.379
Serving on a jury and paying taxes are duties, while volunteering is a choice that builds active citizenship.

67
00:04:53.367 --> 00:04:58.872
It is worth taking part in civic participation, because complaining alone changes nothing.

68
00:04:59.867 --> 00:05:06.835
Not engaging with public life weakens the social contract, the unwritten agreement that citizens help to sustain their community.

69
00:05:07.833 --> 00:05:13.153
Community service and volunteer work show that rights and responsibilities travel together.

70
00:05:14.267 --> 00:05:18.350
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:19.183 --> 00:05:21.345
blank on a jury is a civic duty.

72
00:05:21.645 --> 00:05:25.287
A, Serving. B, To serving. C, Serve.

73
00:05:28.833 --> 00:05:30.705
The answer is A: Serving.

74
00:05:31.850 --> 00:05:34.314
It is no use blank if you do not vote.

75
00:05:34.614 --> 00:05:38.163
A, complain. B, complaining. C, to complaining.

76
00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:43.583
The answer is B: complaining.

77
00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:48.886
The unwritten agreement between citizens and society is the social blank.

78
00:05:49.186 --> 00:05:53.106
A, duty. B, vote. C, contract.

79
00:05:56.667 --> 00:05:58.597
The answer is C: contract.

80
00:05:59.750 --> 00:06:01.483
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:06:01.783 --> 00:06:08.373
A, Not voting is a choice.. B, Not to voting is a choice.. C, No voting is a choice..

82
00:06:11.933 --> 00:06:14.444
The answer is A: Not voting is a choice..

83
00:06:15.583 --> 00:06:19.127
According to the reading, rights and responsibilities travel…

84
00:06:19.427 --> 00:06:22.860
A, apart. B, together. C, slowly.

85
00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:28.202
The answer is B: together.

86
00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:32.308
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:36.810
What civic duty do you think is most important for a healthy society?

88
00:06:45.617 --> 00:06:47.094
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:47.394 --> 00:07:15.082
I think voting is the most important civic duty because it lets citizens choose leaders and hold them accountable. Without participation, governments may ignore ordinary people. However, voting works best together with other duties, such as obeying the law, paying taxes and helping neighbours. A healthy society needs both informed voters and active, caring citizens.

90
00:07:15.382 --> 00:07:17.231
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:18.117 --> 00:07:24.844
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and gerunds as subjects: voting is and it is no use -ing.

92
00:07:25.144 --> 00:07:29.666
Today's value is dedication. Practise a little every day, and you will get better.

93
00:07:29.966 --> 00:07:32.606
See you in Unit eighteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:32.906 --> 00:07:36.104
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

